      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Protease inhibitors (PIs) may be associated with accelerated atherosclerosis in individuals infected with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). We assessed the effects of HIV PIs on subclinical atherosclerosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The lipid profiles, C-reactive protein (CRP) levels, coronary artery calcification (CAC) scores, and blood cell morphologic changes were quantified in 98 black adult participants, aged 25 to 45 years, with HIV-1 infection in Baltimore, Md. Fifty-five participants (56.1%) were taking PIs; 43 participants (43.9%) were not. The Student t and chi(2) tests were used as a means of detecting the between-group differences."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Participants in both the PI and non-PI groups were similar in age, sex, body mass index, blood pressure, heart rate, and red and white blood cell counts. Compared with the non-PI group, the PI group had significantly higher serum total cholesterol (4.8 +/- 1.0 vs 3.8 +/- 0.7 mmol/L, P <.001) and LDL cholesterol (2.9 +/- 0.8 vs 2.1 +/- 0.7 mmol/L, P <.001) levels and red blood cell mean corpuscular volume (92.2 +/- 9.3 vs 86.8 +/- 7.2 microm3, P =.048). The CAC scores in the PI group were also higher than those in the non-PI group (11.0 +/- 28.6 [n = 43] vs 1.7 +/- 5.8, P =.043). CAC scores were marginally associated with log-transformed duration of the PI therapy (P =.055). Serum CRP levels remained unchanged (5.5 +/- 13.6 mg/L [n = 45] vs 3.9 +/- 5.5 mg/L, P =.467). Serum total cholesterol level, LDL cholesterol level, red blood cell mean corpuscular volume, and CAC scores were indicated by means of regression analyses to be associated with log-transformed duration of the PI therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The use of PIs is associated with coronary artery calcification, atherogenic lipid changes, and increased erythrocyte volume in individuals infected with HIV-1."
        }
      ]},
